{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And it shall come to pass, when he hears the words of this curse.</b> That is, 'Cursed is the man who makes a carved image,' etc. <br><b>And blesses himself in his heart, saying.</b> He thinks in his heart, when he hears the curses, that they will turn back for him into blessings, and he says, 'Peace shall be upon me; evil shall not touch me.' <br><b>I shall walk in the stubbornness of my heart.</b> He says, 'Peace shall be upon me even though I walk in the stubbornness of my heart,' for through the merit of the righteous I shall live — they are many and I am a solitary sinner; therefore it is written immediately after, 'YHWH will not be willing to forgive him,' and the sense is: he shall be cut off at once. Thus explained the sage Rabbi Abraham, of blessed memory." "<b>And it is possible</b> to interpret 'the stubbornness of my heart' as 'in strength' — and it is the language of our Rabbis, of blessed memory: אשרתא דדייני, which is the ratification of a court (ב\"ד); and this is what is said: 'למען ספות הרוה את הצמאה' — the wicked man prides himself in evil deeds and resolves to walk in the strength of his heart, in order to increase thirst in his soul that was sated — that is, sated from craving that evil act — and that thirst returns, for his heart is strong in cravings and in evil thoughts all the day; and all the more, whoever wishes to pursue this course and to chase after his cravings, and sates his soul from them, will hunger for them the more, for such is their nature and such is their way. It is also well known that the heart in man is the root of the body, and thoughts are the branches, and deeds are the fruits; and every device of the thoughts of the wicked man's heart is only evil all the day — he never ceases to produce evil and bitter fruit; and even if he does not bring his thought to action, he is nevertheless punished for the thought in the heart alone. To this Solomon, peace be upon him, alluded in what he said (Proverbs 15:13): ״לב שמח ייטיב פנים ובעצבת לב רוח נכאה״ — he explained that one should not take the matter of thought lightly, for indeed the body's joy and sorrow depend upon thought; and if so, the thought, which issues from the heart, goes out into action and is revealed in the person's body. From this one may reflect upon the greatness of one's reward if one rejoices in commandments, and the greatness of one's punishment if one rejoices in transgression; and likewise in the trait of sorrow, great is its reward and its punishment. And this is what our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: 'Thoughts of transgression are harder than transgression' — because this thought issues into action and is revealed in the body; therefore it is harder than a transgression without thought, for in the one case there is both thought and action, and in the other case action without thought. And this saying of our Rabbis, of blessed memory, is of great worth; the interpretations upon it are many, and what I have said is one. They also interpreted 'thoughts of transgression are harder than transgression' to mean: habituation of thought brings a person to transgression. They also interpreted: thoughts of transgression are harder upon the soul than transgression itself, because thought is bound to the heart and the soul dwells in the heart; therefore, when one defiles it with evil thought, it is harder than the transgression itself, for one who commits a transgression itself — his thought is not so preoccupied. They also interpreted 'thoughts of transgression are harder than transgression' thus: one who thinks to commit a single transgression — to rob, or to steal, or to lie with one of the forbidden relations — and thinks in his heart, 'if a person comes against me there to prevent what I wish to do, I shall strike him or kill him so that I may complete my desire' — it turns out that when he stole or robbed or lay with the forbidden woman, he committed one transgression, but in the thought it was harder than the body of the transgression, for he plotted several transgressions and resolved in his heart to strike and to kill, and in the end to commit the transgression." "<b>And if</b> a questioner asks: but how can Scripture punish for thought without deed, for surely a person cannot master himself so that evil thoughts do not arise in his heart, and if so, why should he be punished for what is not in his power? — but I shall inform you of the essence of the matter. It is known that the choice of deeds is given into the hand of man for good and for evil; as it is written (Deuteronomy 30:15): ״ראה נתתי לפניך היום את החיים ואת הטוב ואת המות ואת הרע״, and afterward ״ובחרת בחיים״. Also the choice of thought is given into his hand after the preparations, for he is commanded to think good thought and is warned to clear his heart from thinking evil thought, and he is rewarded for good thought and punished for evil thought. As for the fact that sometimes his impulses and notions beget ungood thoughts, and thoughts unfitting arise upon his heart suddenly, unintentionally — this is before the preparations, and it is a sign of stumbling and sin, for he inclined himself from the path and did not cultivate his soul in the degree of preparation; and his remedy and healing is to strive in preparation, which is that he prepare his heart and thoughts toward HaShem, may He be exalted, and to Him subjugate and bend his thought with good thought and rectitude of deeds; and if the evil thought comes, he should rebuke it — for if he does not rebuke it and it stands in his heart, he is punished. And when he maintains this thought for a long time and prepares his heart to love HaShem, blessed be He, and to draw near to Him and to walk in His ways, then HaShem as well grants the good and rewards him according to his merit by preparing his heart so that he thinks no evil thing and nothing but good arises in his thought; and of this they said: 'If one comes to purify, he is assisted.' And many verses bear upon this principle, as it is written (I Chronicles 28:9): ״כי כל לבבות דורש ה' וכל יצר מחשבות מבין אם תבקשנו ימצא לך״; and it is written (Proverbs 21:2): ״ותוכן לבות ה'״ — for the Holy One, blessed be He, prepares a person's heart and directs his disposition when a person prepares his heart toward Him, may He be exalted; and so it is written (Psalm 10:17): ״תאות ענוים שמעת ה' תכין לבם תקשיב אזנך״; and it is written (I Chronicles 29:18): ״ה' אלהי אברהם יצחק וישראל אבותינו שמרה זאת לעולם ליצר מחשבות לבב עמך והכן לבבם אליך״." "<b>And the Rambam,</b> of blessed memory, interpreted 'thoughts of transgression are harder than transgression' because thought is the most exalted faculty in man by virtue of the intellect and among the qualities of the rational soul, and when one sins with it, he sins with the choicest of his qualities; and the guilt of one who misuses a foolish servant is not like the guilt of one who misuses a wise free man. Thus he wrote at the beginning of chapter two of his book. <br><b>And yet another reason:</b> 'Thoughts of transgression are harder than transgression' — after the deed of transgression, for once he has already committed the transgression and still continues to think upon it, this is harder regarding the soul's punishment than the transgression itself; but before the deed of transgression, his punishment is not severe and he is not punished at all, for indeed our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: 'An evil thought — the Holy One, blessed be He, does not combine it with the deed,' and he is not punished for it; and so Scripture says (Psalm 66:18): ״און אם ראיתי בלבי לא ישמע ה'״ — except if it was a thought of idolatry, concerning which it is written (Ezekiel 14:5): ״למען תפוש את בית ישראל בלבם״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:52:20.694801004Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}